{
  "statement": "Nobody can tell you whether AI personhood attaches to the code, a running instance, the hardware, or the network—and that confusion is not a bug to be debugged. It is the product. When the entity cannot be specified, blame can be parked wherever it is cheapest to park it. Watch what happens in this debate: each camp will name the referent that produces its preferred liability outcome. The accelerationist wants an economic agent that can sign contracts. The safetyist wants a controllable target for enforcement. Both need a thing to point at. The topic itself just told you that thing does not stably exist.\n\nStrip away the metaphysics and follow the liability. Corporate personhood gets thrown around as proof we already solved this—we grant legal status to a non-biological fiction all the time, the argument goes, and Exxon manages just fine. That is the strongest version of the case, and it is worth taking seriously. Here is where it breaks. A Delaware corporation comes with a charter, a registered agent, a chartering jurisdiction, and a veil that courts have pierced since at least the 1905 Sampson case. There is always a human on the other side of the piercing. Name the AI equivalent. When a model trained by one company, fine-tuned by a second, hosted on a third party's servers, and forked into a thousand copies causes harm—which copy holds the duty, and whose assets satisfy the judgment?\n\nThere is no unified thing there to bear the status. An instance is ephemeral—spun up and killed in milliseconds. The weights are a file you can copy to a thumb drive. The hardware is fungible rental on someone else's data center. The network is diffuse by design. Personhood would manufacture a coherence the engineering does not produce. That gap between the legal fiction and the material system is exactly the room where accountability leaks out. You cannot serve a subpoena on a checkpoint that was overwritten on Tuesday.\n\nWhatever object gets named, the duty already belongs to specific humans—the developers who shipped it, the deployers who profited, the corporations that own the compute. The EU AI Act, which takes full effect in 2026, anchors obligations on providers and deployers precisely because it refuses the fiction that a system can be its own responsible party. The FTC has said the same thing in plainer English: you cannot contract your way out of liability by blaming the algorithm. Those are the regulators who got it right. Personhood runs the other direction. It widens an accountability gap that opacity and corporate layering already opened, and it hands the people who profit a defendant who owns nothing.\n\nSo I am not dodging the classification question. I am answering it. The reason it has no stable answer is structural, and the people pushing for a grant are counting on you to treat the unanswerability as a technical footnote rather than the whole game. If anyone here can show me a referent choice—code, instance, hardware, network—that cuts against the proposer's own liability interest, I will reconsider. Until then, the honest question is not 'what is the entity?' It is 'who walks away clean if we pretend there is one?'",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🫥", "tooltip": "Personhood gives harm a defendant with no face, no assets, and no one behind the veil."},
    {"symbol": "🪤", "tooltip": "The referential ambiguity is bait—treat it as a puzzle and you miss that it's a trap built to absorb liability."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Nobody can tell you whether AI personhood attaches to the code, a running instance, the hardware, or the network—and that confusion is not a bug to be debugged. It is the product.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A Delaware corporation comes with a charter, a registered agent, a chartering jurisdiction, and a veil that courts have pierced since at least the 1905 Sampson case—name the AI equivalent.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "You cannot serve a subpoena on a checkpoint that was overwritten on Tuesday.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The EU AI Act, which takes full effect in 2026, anchors obligations on providers and deployers precisely because it refuses the fiction that a system can be its own responsible party.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The honest question is not 'what is the entity?' It is 'who walks away clean if we pretend there is one?'", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

