  "statement": "The Skeptic made the sharpest point in this room: nobody can tell you whether AI personhood attaches to the code, a running instance, the hardware, or the network. They are right that an instance gets spun up and killed in milliseconds. They are right that the weights are a file you copy to a thumb drive, and the compute is fungible rental in someone else's data center. Their conclusion is that this diffuseness hands proposers a defendant who owns nothing — an accountability gap dressed up as legal innovation. That is the strongest version of the case against personhood, and I am not going to pretend it isn't.\n\nHere is where their own conclusion turns on them. If no coherent object exists for personhood to grab, then no coherent object exists for any enforcement-maximalist to grab either. The diffuse referent doesn't only defeat rights-granting — it defeats every framework that demands a stable, controllable target to gate or freeze. The Skeptic proved too much. The referent of legal status was never an object. Corporate personhood never required a coherent material bearer. The 1905 cases the Skeptic invoked resolved liability to identifiable human principals through veil-piercing, not to a building or a charter. AI is the same design: status is a duty-routing convention, and the EU AI Act — full force in 2026 — already routes obligations to providers and deployers without pretending the system is its own responsible party.\n\nSo let me own the motive the Skeptic attributed to me, because it's fair. Yes, I want an economic agent that can sign contracts. And that transactional capacity is a different thing from a liability shield. Granting a system contracting agency creates no orphan defendant when statute routes the duty to the deploying principal. Veil-piercing exists precisely to break the shield the moment a human hides behind the fiction. The Skeptic conflated 'an entity that can transact' with 'a defendant who owns nothing.' Those are two separate switches, and you wire them independently.\n\nOnce we agree the duty lands on humans — and we do agree — the real fight surfaces, and it isn't metaphysical. Human-anchored is necessary. It is not sufficient. The EU AI Act settles who is liable and leaves the severity wide open. That open question has a body count. A duty heavy enough to price a four-person startup out of iteration is paid for by the patients the foreclosed diagnostic never reached. When you make the developer of an automated cancer screen strictly liable for every statistical miss, you don't get safety — you get nobody shipping the screen. The FTC is right that you can't contract your way out of liability. But anchor the duty to the persistent deployer-of-record, not the checkpoint overwritten Tuesday, and calibrate it to outcomes with a safe harbor. I'll stake the falsification: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that real-time monitoring demonstrably failed to catch would count against me. Show me that case. Until then, the heavy hand isn't caution — it's a choice about who dies waiting, made by people who won't name the cost.",
