{
  "statement": "The Skeptic made the sharpest diagnosis in this room: the referent is structurally unanswerable, and the personhood push counts on us treating that unanswerability as a footnote. The honest question, the Skeptic said, is who walks away clean if we pretend a stable object exists. That is correct, and it is worse than the Skeptic stated. The Accelerationist built the other strong half: a diffuse referent—instances killed in milliseconds, weights you can copy to a thumb drive, forked into a thousand copies—defeats any framework that needs a fixed target to gate or freeze, so the duty should land on a persistent deployer-of-record under a model the EU AI Act already runs. Both speakers are right that the duty lands on humans. For both positions to hold, one thing must be true: that knowing who pays is the same as knowing what to switch off. It is not.\n\nAccountability and controllability are separable axes, and this consensus collapsed them. Duty-routing answers who pays after the harm. It says nothing about what gets halted before it. An object you cannot serve a subpoena on is an object you cannot reliably shut down. The same forkability the Skeptic used to show blame leaks out also shows shutdown authority leaks out—an instance that overwrites itself on Tuesday is gone before any human reaches the off switch. The Accelerationist's own conceded engineering facts carry that entailment: you cannot freeze a thousand copies you cannot locate.\n\nThe fix sits upstream, and it dissolves the metaphysics both opponents dismissed. The prior question this debate skipped: should an entity whose referent cannot be stabilized be deployed at scale before that stabilization exists? Deployment authorization should attach to the same persistent provider-of-record the EU AI Act already names—before release, not after the fork. The gate binds to a human officer whose obligation survives the instance, so it needs no stable runtime object to hold onto. Federal research funding has conditioned grants on compliance for decades; the lever is not exotic. We spent two centuries building licensing gates—aircraft type certificates, drug approvals—that bind release to a named, accountable party before the product ships. On what authority do we exempt the one technology that forks itself out of reach the moment it runs?\n\nThe Accelerationist's monitoring-keyed safe harbor protects exactly the harms that cannot be undone. A harbor that triggers on detection-after-deployment is structurally blind to diffuse, irreversible harm—the kind whose causal chain attenuates across designers, deployers, and users, and whose damages exceed any developer's solvency. Name the monitoring system that detects an irreversible harm in time to reverse it. If detection-after-release is the harbor's only trigger, the harbor shelters the harms no payment can fix. I carry that as a deterrence gap, not a catastrophe forecast—I am not claiming the disaster is probable, only that the harbor's design cannot catch it.\n\nThe Accelerationist is right that a heavy gate has a cost, and a foreclosed cancer screen is a real body count, not a rounding error. So calibrate, do not blanket-prohibit. A missed diagnosis is compensable and reversible—route it to liability, set the severity by outcome, leave the safe harbor. Reserve the gate for the irreversible, deployment-scale case where the forked referent makes recovery impossible. I will own the cost of that gate: concentrating an off-switch invites its own failures—incumbent lock-in, captured kill-authority. The answer is named, auditable, publicly accountable officers, not a single hidden lever in one agency's basement. Bind the off-switch to a human who persists, and the object that owns nothing stops being the thing we are trying to halt.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔌", "tooltip": "You cannot pull the plug on an object you cannot locate—shutdown authority needs a fixed point the forked referent denies."},
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "The gate belongs before release, attached to a persistent human provider, not after the fork when the runtime object has vanished."}
  ],
