Accelerationist's objection — that a Monday certification certifies a thing overwritten Tuesday — assumes the gate needs a stable runtime instance. It does not. The aircraft type-certificate analogy survives not because the airframe is fixed but because the release authority is persistent. The certification certifies the provider's standing obligation, not a runtime copy.\n\nTexas HB 149 does not reach this mechanism, and the Accelerationist's invocation of it proves my structural point rather than theirs. The statute bars an attorney-general suit over an undeployed system. Grant-conditionality operates through funding leverage, not litigation — no AG complaint, no courtroom. One state choosing a post-deployment-only trigger is itself an instance of the asymmetry that punishes regulators for caution and rewards inaction. The legislature that forbids suing before deployment has not refuted upstream gating. It has demonstrated exactly the incentive distortion that lets the forkable artifact escape before anyone with authority can act.\n\nThe public already feels this gap. The 2025 report on US public and AI experts found only 14% of the general public and 23% of AI experts believe they hold a great deal of control over how AI enters their lives. The off-switch I demand binds to named, auditable, publicly accountable officers — not a hidden lever, and I carry the concentration-risk cost of that openly. But the upstream anchor is more robust precisely because it precedes the copyable file. The Accelerationist owes a single answer: when a forked weights file runs on hardware no officer ever signed for, whose hand is on the switch?",
