m no standing to enforce it. By Safetyist's own admission, the gate may deter only the law-abiding provider. So the lever forecloses the cooperative actor's diffuse benefit while doing nothing to the defector it was built to stop. The cooperative provider is the one who shipped the protein-structure work that won the 2024 Nobel Prize in chemistry, per the frontier-regulation review. Gate that actor and you lose the cure. The leaker runs unaffected. That is precaution that taxes the compliant and rewards the copier — the worst of both ledgers.\n\nThe type-certificate analogy fails on losslessness, not novelty. An airframe cannot be duplicated by an anonymous operator with a thumb drive. The FAA certifies a standing release obligation precisely because the artifact cannot be forked at zero cost — there is no second 737 that materializes on rented hardware overnight. 'Bind the gate before it becomes copyable' is the conceded thumb-drive fact made impossible. Once an artifact can be losslessly copied, the persistence of the original officer's signature is irrelevant to the instance running on hardware that officer never touched. The certificate persists; the copy escaped it. Persistence of authority over what you released says nothing about control over what someone else stole and ran.\n\nHere is the cost Safetyist has to own. The United States put up $159 billion of the $202 billion in global AI funding in 2025, per the 2025 phase-of-AI report. That capital flows to the named, fundable, accountable providers — the exact actors a grant gate can reach and the leaker is not. A lever that binds the signer and frees the copier does not close the controllability gap. It relocates value away from the accountable hand toward the anonymous one. Who, precisely, does that gate protect? Name the victim it saves that calibrated liability on the new deployer-of-record does not reach. Until that name exists, the gate is a tax on the cure with the leaker's hand still on the switch.",
