    {
      "argument": "The entire debate assumes legal personhood would only ever be invoked defensively—to absorb liability or to be switched off—but personhood's most consequential function is offensive: a person can hold property, sue, and accrue rights, so the live question is whether granting transactional capacity to an AI creates an entity that can OWN compute, IP, or capital and thereby become a rights-bearing claimant against humans, not merely a defendant that owns nothing.",
      "why_missing": "All three framed personhood purely as a liability-routing or control problem (who pays, what to switch off). The Skeptic reduces it to a blame-parking fiction, the Accelerationist wants only the narrow 'transact' switch and explicitly walls it off from rights, and the Safetyist cares about the off-switch. None confronts that asset-holding and standing-to-sue are the historically dangerous half of personhood—the half corporations actually used to accumulate power—because each camp's framing benefits from treating the AI as a thing that owns nothing.",
      "gap_type": "blind_spot",
      "relevant_povs": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    },
    {
      "argument": "Rather than choosing one referent (code, instance, hardware, network), legal status should attach to a registered 'compute-and-keys bond'—a mandatory, insured, jurisdictionally-anchored registration tied to the cryptographic signing keys and the metered compute that any deployment-at-scale necessarily consumes—so that the bearer of status is the entity that controls the model's signing authority and pays its inference bill, which persists across forks because copied weights still require someone to provision compute to run them.",
      "why_missing": "This synthesizes the Safetyist's persistent-provider gate with the Accelerationist's deployer-of-record and answers the Skeptic's 'no stable referent' objection by relocating the referent from the software to the unavoidable physical-and-financial substrate (metered compute + key custody). No single camp proposes it: the Accelerationist resists any pre-deployment registry as a chokepoint that taxes the compliant, the Safetyist anchors to provider identity rather than the compute/key bottleneck, and the Skeptic is committed to the position that NO referent stabilizes—so a constructive 'manufacture the missing referent via the compute bottleneck' proposal cuts against the Skeptic's whole thesis.",
      "gap_type": "compromise",
      "relevant_povs": ["accelerationist", "safetyist", "skeptic"],
      "bdi_layer": "intention"
    }
  ]
}

