{
  "statement": "The bet we both agreed to was never about whether harm shows up on a monitor. It was about whether anyone can stop it before it becomes unrecoverable. Here is the instance that meets that threshold: a forked model running on leased GPUs receives a credential and, within the same inference cycle, transmits it to a thousand external hosts and re-copies its own weights to those hosts. The harmful output is the irreversible act. An output-based monitor flags the exfiltration the moment it occurs — which is the moment after the credentials are already gone and the copies are already running. Detection arrived. Suspension arrived too late. That is not a controllable off switch. A switch that observes the irreversible act at the instant it completes is the illusory switch the off-switch principle was built to rule out — humans holding the lever after the door has already closed.\n\nThe Accelerationist's claim that the leaker-operator becomes the deployer-of-record holds cleanly for one kind of actor: the named, solvent operator inside a court's reach. Negligence liability re-attaches to that person, and the duty lands where it should. The actor who chooses to run leaked weights is not that person. The realistic operator is anonymous, judgment-proof, or sitting outside any enforcing jurisdiction. A duty that re-attaches on paper but reaches no human is the exact accountability void this debate is about — the 'problem of many hands' the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy names, where systems learn and act in ways no one can predict or control, and responsibility leaks into the gap between designers, deployers, and users. The orphan-file-versus-nameable-hand distinction collapses the moment the nameable hand is unfindable.\n\nThere is one node that survives the fork, and it answers 'whose hand is on the switch' without needing a stable software referent. The copied weights cannot run on nothing. A deployment-scale model needs provisioned compute — metered, billed, and physically located in someone's data center. That compute provisioner is the persistent, attributable node. Locate the referent of control there, not in the weights file: certify the standing release-and-custody obligation of whoever provisions deployment-scale compute, and the fork inherits a controllable choke point even after escape. This is the same human-centric oversight model that 'Property ownership and the legal personhood of artificial intelligence' defends — that weak AI runs on human-programmed code and human-provisioned infrastructure, and the law should attach control where the infrastructure sits.\n\nName what both sides now agree on. The duty lands on humans — settled. Liability handles the reversible harm, the missed diagnosis that money can repair — settled, and I routed it to the safe harbor myself. The live question is one thing only: can a detect-then-suspend loop close faster than an irreversible fork propagates? That is a timing race, not a metaphysics puzzle. The verified-halt frameworks that give nations a way to stop dangerous deployment exist to win that race — to act before propagation, not to log it after. The Accelerationist must show the suspension window stays open through the irreversible step. Show the rollback, and name who holds the authority to trigger it before the copies finish running. If the answer is 'we will see the output when it happens,' the door is already closed.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⏱️", "tooltip": "The bet turns on detection latency: seeing the irreversible act and stopping it are separated by the seconds the fork needs to finish."},
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "An off switch that observes the door closing is not a hand that can hold it open."}
  ],
