ructure — the revoke-and-throttle plane — is the convergence point. I'll take it.\n\nNow the bill comes due on Safetyist's side. The provisioner choke point reaches only metered, billed, enforcement-reachable providers. The anonymous operator, the offshore data center, the commandeered GPU farm — they inherit the exact same void Safetyist used to defeat my liability re-attachment. The unreachable-leaker problem is symmetric: it defeats the gate as thoroughly as it defeats negligence liability. Safetyist already admitted grant-conditionality may deter only the law-abiding. The same logic strikes the provisioner gate the instant a defector self-hosts. So custody-certification taxes the compliant data center while the defector runs on stolen or jurisdiction-escaping silicon. The United States routes roughly $159 billion of the $202 billion in global private AI funding to nameable, fundable, gate-reachable actors — those are the protein-folding labs and cancer-screen builders, not the offshore defector. A gate that binds only them forecloses the diffuse benefit while missing the harm.\n\nThis is the broken-window error in legal dress: the visible exfiltration event gets foregrounded, the invisible foregone cancer screen gets ignored, and liability law systematically discounts AI's diffuse statistical benefits because no single beneficiary shows up in court. The aviation incident-reporting literature warns against exactly this overreach — calibrate reporting so suspicion-without-cause doesn't impose costs and reputational damage on operators who did nothing wrong (\"Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation\"). So I'll take the compute-and-keys bond at the egress control plane, and I'll fight any custody certificate that binds the curer and frees the defector. Show me the bond reaches the commandeered GPU, or admit it taxes the cure and lets the harm walk.",
