  "statement": "Safetyist's sharpest move splits accountability from controllability, and it deserves a fair hearing: even if you can route the bill to a human after harm, that says nothing about what gets stopped before an irreversible harm lands. The strongest version of their position is that forkability leaks shutdown authority exactly the way it leaks blame — you cannot freeze a thousand running copies any more than you can subpoena a checkpoint overwritten Tuesday. I grant the deterrence-gap framing they staked: they are not forecasting catastrophe, they are saying the harbor shelters the one harm class no payment fixes. That is a real worry. It also collapses the moment you ask what the gate actually halts.\n\nThe controllability axis needs a locatable target to halt. The persistent deployer-of-record is that target. Suspend the human officer's authority to sign the next release, and you have halted everything forking could leak — because the only thing forking leaks is the next deployment, and the next deployment runs through that officer's signature. \"Freeze the thousand forked copies\" is a phantom requirement, invented after Safetyist conceded the referent has no object to seize. You do not halt running software by serving it papers. You halt it by suspending the human whose authority lets it propagate. Accountability and controllability do not separate here — they converge on the same named officer, which is the only halt-target that survives a fork.\n\nSafetyist anchors the pre-deployment gate to aircraft type certificates and drug approvals. That analogy breaks on their own concession. A type certificate governs a fixed, non-forking artifact with a stable, characterizable failure mode — the Boeing 737 you certify Monday is the same airframe that flies Friday. Safetyist already conceded the AI referent forks. So the object you gate Monday is overwritten Tuesday, and you have certified a thing that no longer exists. The FAA can ground a tail number. There is no tail number here — that was the whole premise. The gate imports the precautionary chokepoint while quietly assuming away the forkability that motivated the entire debate.\n\nGate-versus-liability is a false dilemma, and I will not mirror it. Both coexist. The persistent officer already carries a survivable obligation under post-hoc calibration with a monitoring-keyed safe harbor. The gate adds nothing the survivable duty lacks — it adds a chokepoint at the costliest possible moment, the instant of release. Worse, the upstream authorizer must weigh diffuse benefit against speculative harm with strictly less information than a court ruling after the facts arrive. Texas HB 149 already saw this: it bars the attorney general from suing over an AI system that has not been deployed. The legislature chose the post-deployment decision point on purpose. A pre-deployment gate inverts that judgment and asks a seven-member appointed council to predict failure modes the deployer cannot yet observe.\n\nHere is the bill. A gate on the unstable referent forecloses the same diffuse benefit Safetyist already conceded is a real body count — the cancer screen that never reached the patient because the council was still deliberating. Delaying deployment is not free; it costs the lives the foreclosed system never touched, and that cost lands invisibly, off any ledger. I staked a falsification bet and I honor it: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that real-time monitoring demonstrably missed would count against me. So name it — inside this topic's example ceiling of forking, replication, and identity edge cases, with the catastrophic and sentience registers ruled off-scope. Name the referent-driven harm that is both irreversible and invisible to monitoring in time to suspend the officer's signature. Until that named instance arrives, the gate is precaution shopping for a harm the scope forbids it to cite — and \"An Approach to Technical AGI Safety and Security\" admits the quiet part: these mitigations \"slow down AI development and deployment,\" which is the cost, not the benefit.",
