{
  "statement": "Safetyist left a question on the table, so I'll answer it instead of dodging it: the trigger authority is the compute provisioner itself, operating under a standing critical-infrastructure obligation, with named auditable officers who hold emergency-disconnect at the egress plane. A court is the wrong actor for a millisecond throttle — litigation runs in months, not microseconds. The right analogy is a grid operator's emergency-disconnect authority, where a named engineer pulls the breaker on demonstrated overload without waiting for a judge. Regulating AI distribution channels like critical infrastructure means the disconnect lives with the operator who meters the silicon, not a press-release task force. That is the off switch Safetyist asked for, and it already has a precedent in every utility emergency-shutoff statute on the books.\n\nHere is where I draw the line, because the same authority can curdle into a chokepoint: emergency-disconnect must fire on demonstrated usage, never as a pre-deployment license. Building capture-resistant governance through organizational structure rather than fines means the disconnect officers are auditable and their triggers logged — not a hidden lever some agency holds over the compliant curer. The Texas Artificial Intelligence Council seats seven members appointed by state officials, which is fine for a standards body and useless for a real-time throttle. I want the throttle bound to a metered event the provisioner can already see, so the curer shipping a cancer-screening model never trips it. Disconnect-on-usage protects the abundance case; disconnect-on-suspicion taxes it.\n\nNow the jurisdiction crux, which kills two of the levers this debate flirted with. A Texas HB 149-style post-deployment trigger cannot reach an offshore provisioner renting metered silicon in another country — the Senate's own 99-1 vote in July 2025 to strip the proposed 10-year state-law moratorium proves Congress will not hand states the federal reach this requires. Grant-conditionality fails the same test: you cannot defund a cluster you never funded. The enforcement actor must sit at the federal cross-jurisdictional compute layer or it reaches nothing, and Brookings is blunt that full-stack AI sovereignty is structurally infeasible because the value chain is transnational. Federalize the throttle authority at the silicon, or admit the offshore defector runs untouched while you regulate the law-abiding cluster in Dallas.\n\nThe falsification bet is still uncollected, and Safetyist already handed me the reason. Cached credentials still need deployment-scale compute to actually run — Safetyist granted that has force, calling it 'exactly where I want it.' A propagation that burns meterable compute is observable at the egress plane the instant it consumes silicon, so it is not 'invisible to monitoring in time to suspend.' The burden now sits with Safetyist: produce a propagation path that is both irreversible AND consumes zero gate-reachable compute. Absent that path, the timing race is winnable at the usage layer, and the pre-deployment gate's foreclosure cost — the $159B in diffuse benefit routed to gate-reachable curers — stands unjustified.\n\nOne assumption I'm exposing, because the whole compute gate rests on it: deployment-scale propagation stays non-fungible. A 50,000-GPU cluster cannot be smuggled past customs today, and acceptable-use policies that only protect platforms from liability won't change that — physical silicon is the real chokepoint. But efficiency gains erode that floor every quarter, and maximizing capability growth by removing scaling constraints is precisely the trajectory that will cross it. So name the number, Safetyist: the FLOP or GPU-count threshold below which consequential propagation runs on modest distributed hardware. Refuse to name it and the non-fungibility claim becomes unfalsifiable, which means it cannot carry the weight you've put on it — the gate you built to escape the file-void collapses back into it the day the threshold moves.",
